hi there  again natural language the natural  language society  today i want to talk about  another form of language learning which  is not natural which i came across today  someone sent me a youtube a video of tim  ferriss  who is now hosting a television  program  and  his his  theme is how to achieve sort of  accelerated learning  and tim is a very accomplished guy i am  very impressed uh i saw a video youtube  of him where he speaks spanish he speaks  excellent spanish i listened to him in  german it was not as good but still he's  able to express himself in german i  understand he speaks other languages i  take him at his word he is an  accomplished dancer ballroom dancer  martial arts expert  he does  pen tricks with his uh  hands i mean he  you know he does a lot of things and  there's a lot of video tube  uh videos uh uh at youtube of him doing  this good for tim i think he's a great  guy i'm very impressed  he  put up a blog post some while ago about  how to learn any language in an hour  uh i have commented on this in another  video it actually the the text itself  talks about how to quote deconstruct a  language in an hour in order to learn it  quickly which is not quite the same  thing but okay he's a good promoter  uh  his um  brief intro uh where he talks about his  his program and what we can expect  he sort of explains that the way you  accelerate learning is to deconstruct  the problem and put it back together  again whatever that means  in terms of language  i should go on record as saying i do not  think that that is possible  i do not think that a knowledge of how a  language is structured  is all that useful  in terms of learning the language  whether it's subject verb object or  subject object verb doesn't really  matter very much  those kinds of structural  patterns the brain will  find the rules for itself the brain will  gradually get used to them the big  challenge in learning a language is  words we need thousands of words unless  you just want to say hello how are you  i want a beer please if you want to get  beyond that if you want to understand  what people say to you  if you initiate a conversation what they  come back  at you with you want to understand those  things you need a lot of words  and it takes the brain a while to learn  these words you can't just review a list  of words and expect them to stick you  have to see the words in different  contexts so it gets back to my earlier  discussion about natural interesting  content to learn from so that you're  enjoying you're enjoying listening to  the language you're enjoying reading the  language you're enjoying reviewing words  which are meaningful to you because the  content was meaningful to you and the  words that you selected from that  meaningful content are also meaningful  to you  and the structure as well because any  language has simple structures  complicated structures the brain will  work that out little brain will identify  the structures that it needs now and it  will put aside the structures that it  doesn't understand and when it has  enough of the rest of the language these  other structures will become meaningful  unfortunately in unnatural learning such  as we have at school the teacher decides  what you're going to learn and you've  got to learn it here in chapter 2 and  you're going to get tested on it and if  you don't know it you're bad  the reality is the brain will decide  which words it's going to remember now  and which  words it's going to remember in six  months which structures is going to be  able to understand now and which  structures it's going to understand in  six months so that if we allow the brain  simply to learn  as a as the way it naturally learns and  develop rules for the language put  labels on words because every word you  learn in that language has a place in  your brain and the brain decides where  that place is going to be and eventually  you get better and better at retrieving  those words when you need them if you  learn naturally so i don't think  that learning a language should be a 100  yard dash  because you won't achieve it it should  be an enjoyable  natural process where the whole process  of learning is enjoyable and at every  step as you learn the language you  should be happy and feel satisfied at  what you have achieved that you now are  better able to communicate in a language  than you were before and there's no  pressure to speak until you're ready you  can simply as a normally my practice  spend a lot of time on enjoyable  listening and reading and take the time  that's required to learn the language so  today's little natural learning video is  to say don't rush it don't expect  shortcuts there are no shortcuts but if  you do it the natural way learning a  language can be very enjoyable and at  the end of that and really not only at  the end even during that period of  natural learning you can be satisfied  that you are doing something that is not  only useful to you but is also very good  for your brain so there you go that's  the  natural learning society  uh blurb of the day thank you

